API-28: round control times set through the API to the nearest second

setcontrol(), used by EN_addcontrol and EN_setcontrol, converts the time
of a TIMER or TIMEOFDAY control with (long)level, which truncates. A
time computed in hours by the caller, such as 8.2 * 3600 =
29519.999999999996, became 29519 s, so the control acted a second
early. Before commit a9079b0 the API rounded these times. Round again,
and first refuse (error 202) a time that is negative, not a number, or
too large for a long, such as 1e300, whose conversion was undefined
behaviour. The limit is half the range of a long, as for [TIMES].

Requires: API-22

Apply from the root of an EPANET checkout at 83e25bb, after API-22:
    git apply API-22_fix.patch
    git apply API-28_fix.patch

diff --git a/src/project.c b/src/project.c
index 8b89c41..9b831b2 100644
--- a/src/project.c
+++ b/src/project.c
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 */
 
 #include <stdlib.h>
+#include <limits.h>
 #include <stdio.h>
 #include <string.h>
 #include <math.h> 
@@ -1377,8 +1378,11 @@ int setcontrol(EN_Project p, int type, int linkIndex, double setting,
     }
     else if (type == TIMER || type == TIMEOFDAY)
     {
-        t = (long)level;
-        if (t < 0) return 202;
+        // Time in seconds must be a number from 0 up to half the range
+        // of a long, so that times can be added without overflow (NaN
+        // fails this test)
+        if (!(level >= 0.0 && level < LONG_MAX / 2.0)) return 202;
+        t = (long)(level + 0.5);
         if (type == TIMEOFDAY) t %= SECperDAY;
     }
     
